The swift mouths of Gabriel's host cannot grasp it,
and from the beauty of Michael's host it is very far.
Lofty and exalted, remote and concealed, hidden and high;
strong and sublime, veiled and glorified, mighty and wondrous.
He holds the depths and grasps the ends by the power of his being;
he clings to creatures and nourishes the worlds by his works;
all creatures are full of him, yet he lacks nothing.
All the ends are contained in him, and in him they are made complete;
greater than the height, he dwells in the depth and wholly in all things.
On the summit of the height he sits like a judge,
and at his command the orders of all creatures move.
His power spread out the earth by a sign, upon nothing,
and like a mighty man the strength of his creative power carries it.
Under his command creation is supported so that it does not sink,
and above, his sign has bound the heavens so that they are not rolled up.
He dwells like a soul within creatures and carries them;
in him they are supported and not far from his ordering.
He is beyond them, and here among them, and within them all;
in him they are contained, for he is the boundary of all their limits.

look upon His Shekhinah, how fearful it is over His creatures.
This is the power your Betrothed has in His glory;
do not draw back from His majesty in your fear.
Establish with Him the perfect covenant for which He came;
let me not be put to shame in you, for I have guarded you since Egypt."
The congregation trembles, and Moses hastens to encourage her;
the mountain smokes, the height is mighty, Sinai burns.
Fearful voices, wondrous visions, rushing thunders;
ordered ranks, extended rows, glorious hosts;
encircling troops, gathered thousands, joined armies;
Seraphim sanctifying, Cherubim hovering, Watchers flying;
a wondrous sight, a marvelous likeness, a hidden Shekhinah;
a standing cloud, a burning fire, a cry from on high;
spread-out splendor, poured-out light, streaming glory;
stretched-out brightness, mighty voice, rising clamor.
Moses humble, the Bridegroom mighty, the bride contemptuous;
the chamber decked out, the tent pitched, beauty splendid;
the groomsmen reclining, the worlds rejoicing, the wedding prepared,
and the bride withdrawing from the feast.
The scribe stands and holds the reed to write the covenant,
and the Bridegroom waits to set the world in order by His providence.

when waves of offense blew against Him from every side;
when He was reproached for eating with sinners;
when they rebuked Him for mingling even with tax collectors;
when they demanded of Him the poll tax to give to the king;
when He had nowhere there to lay His head;
when He was carried alone on a bare colt;
when He was girded with a towel about His loins in the likeness of a servant,
when He washed the feet of dust that His hands had fashioned;
when He was sold by those of His household who went with Him,
when even the true ones who clung to Him denied Him;
when He was questioned by the judge like a murderer,
when He was scourged with whips like a guilty man;
when He was bound to a pillar in the court and drank the lashes,
when He was clothed by madmen in garments of mockery;
when on His head was a crown of thorns that the defiled had woven,
when He was slapped and asked who had struck Him;
when He carried the wood of the cross on His shoulder and went out,
when His hands and feet were nailed by those who had gone astray;
when lots fell over His garments, to whom they should go,
when bitter gall and vinegar were held out to Him on a sponge;
when His side was pierced by a merciless spear,
when He was embalmed and wrapped and buried—blessed is her discernment!

behold, he is in heaven, in his place, exalted, glorious and praised:
above the mountains, above the expanses, above the heights,
above the archons, above the glory of the rulers;
above the hosts, above the ranks, above the orders,
above the legions, above the thousands beyond counting;
in the place higher than the angels and their hosts,
in the height hidden even from the Watchers and their acclamations;
where there is no room for the Watchers to minister,
nor does any spirit dare to enter and hover;
in the place higher than the Chariot and its whirling wheels,
where neither the yoked creatures nor the winged ones travel;
in the holy of holies, where the Trinity dwells,
which not even the Seraphim, though they veil themselves, come near;
where fire, if it drew near, would at once be burned,
and flame would tremble if it entered;
in the place where the light of sun and moon outside its gate
is held of no account and cast aside, lest it be sullied by their rising;
in a tabernacle not made by hands, not composed of creatures,
where the One dwells who prepared baptism for you.
To this assembly your mind is bound when you call

Your word, our Lord, is full of light for the one who loves it;
give me your word, that I may walk by it without stumbling.
The world is dark, and you are radiant, Son of God;
in you, my Lord, let me walk, for you dearly love the one who clings to you.
The road of the world is tangled (and full of every stumbling-block;
woe to the one who walks in it, for how wretched he is!

Open my lips to speak of your story with love;
Son of God, grant me to tell of you richly.
Through you I make bold to steal all treasures from you;
Lord of the treasuries, give me your riches, that I may be filled with them.
Reveal your mysteries to me, that I may reveal them to the whole earth,
so that all of it may sing praise to your Godhead.
Yours are all the riches that your Father has;
grant me from them to grow rich, and to enrich whoever listens to me.
Give me your word, which is wealth to whoever acquires it;
I will acquire it, and make it the possession of whoever is eager for gain.
I will gather the rose of your words on my tongue from your readings,
and bring them out and scatter them sweetly among the assemblies of your stories.
The invited guests are waiting to drink life from your bowl;
let your cup of life make drunk the assembly that longs for you.
Your good wine came forth to creation at the end of the ages,
the wine your Father had kept to be the comfort of the world.

At the hour of your judgment, Son of God, listen to my mind;
command me to speak of your humiliation as I suffer.
My thoughts have entered the house of judgment to hear you;
there grant me to see the wonder of your humility.
The house of the crucifiers pressed upon my mind, and it stood gazing at you;
grant it to tell the story of your judgment richly.
Hidden and spiritual knowledge saw you before the judge,
and marveled at you, that you stood there so calmly.

and, as if with words, in silence he rebuked the chief of the disciples.
He looked at Simon and spoke to him like this:
"Why, Simon, do you deny me in the time of suffering?
Woe to you, my disciple! Where is the love of your apostleship?
Chief of the disciples, why do you flee in the time of ordeal?
When I was giving blessing to you all, you were first;
when I was promising thrones, again you were the head.
You had never fled from me until today, O disciple;
in the ordeal you have left me—where, then, is your love for me?
When, Simon, did you not know me, until today?
And where did you forget me, if not here, where I am mocked?
When the waves of the sea were raging at you, you ran to me;
and now that the scribes cover my face, see, you deny.
When I was turning water into wine, you were near;
and now that the priests spit on me, see, you are far off.
When I multiplied bread in the wilderness, you were giving the orders;
and now that the people have prepared bitter things, you are nowhere to be seen.
When Elijah and Moses were on the mountain, you did not forget me;
but because pain surrounds me here, see, you deny.
Your love is insipid if you change with the times,

"I beg you, my Lord, let me not be estranged from your company;
wickedness has driven me out—let the love of your sweetness bring me in.
Sin lay in ambush for me and threw me down in ruin without pity;
you, the wise one, bind up the wound that has been dealt me.
The waves of wickedness surrounded me on dry land to drown me;
you, my Lord, draw me out, as you rescue from floods.
These sufferings, see, you bear for the sake of the wicked;
show your grace in me first by pardoning me.
In this judgment you stand for the sake of sinners;
revive me, the sinner, first, for I have asked it of you.
I denied, and I am the one who now confesses;
receive my confession and blot out the bond of denial.
Where is your grace to be proved, if not in me,
for more than all I have worked wickedness in iniquity?
See, the great whirlpool of wickedness has swallowed me, our Lord;
give me your hand, that I may come up out of it and not perish in it.
I have fallen into a pit, and I cannot climb up from the depths;
stretch out your mercies to me, my Lord, to draw me out.
The evil one has hunted me, and in his snare he is strangling me;
break his snare, and I will escape from the ravager."

We have pressed hard after excessive foods;
let us fast today, lest we sink our mind in filth.
We have run hard with the body after provisions;
let us now seek the life of the soul, to gain it.
We have greatly loved to gather bodily things;
let us toil a little in spiritual things while they may be found.
We have taken much delight in food, which is all filth;
let us now toil a little in fasting; it will not be heavy for us.
See, foods and delicacies are left to corruption;
why are they so eagerly run after, when they are nothing?
The belly took all our running, and it was spoiled within it;
it is Sheol, which rots every morsel given to it.
All the year we gave all our labour to the body;
let us now give the soul something to comfort her.
The body's delicacies grew, spoiled and became filth;
let us now taste fasting, which never spoils.
If the body is the soul's partner, why does it cheat her,
and take all the running of diligence for itself?
Warm clothing, fine bread and sweet wine,
meat and oil and abundant delicacies.

Great are you, O fast, and great is the mystery that is served in you,
for in you all victories are given to all the victorious.
In you the great prophet, son of the Levites, became radiant,
and became for the people a sun of flesh, clothed in splendour.
In you he went within and touched the depths of the Godhead,
and came out and proclaimed life to the world with great hope.
In you Elijah was made fair and pure and gained wings,
and flew up and ascended to the place that death does not enter.
In you our Saviour cast down our Adversary in battle,
and at your completion He laid waste Sheol for our sake.
The fast is armour, and even if a weak man puts it on,
he grows strong [?] in it and is not defeated when he fights.
Arise like strong shields, you who have understood what the fast is,
and run it rightly, according to the form of the fast:
in faith, which is the mystery of all good things;
in love of humankind, which brings you near to God;
in armour forged of prayers and almsgiving;
in righteousness on the right hand and on the left;
in humility, which turns its cheek to the one who strikes it;
in pure love, which is not angry with its neighbour;
in gifts that go out from your threshold to whoever is in need,
and in alms that fly from you to the beggars;

O rich man, acquire mercy in the world;
and you, poor man, see that you do not murmur against the Creator.
Praised be the Creator who made riches and poverty;
from the rich and from the poor be praise to him.

I renounce the world, its despised wealth and its desires,
and also its possessions, its name, its glory and its power.
I renounce palaces, if they are far from converse with you,
and citadels, if they are not rich in your cross.
Acquire me, you who redeemed me; take me captive, O Captor, since you let me go;
enter, bring me out of Pharaoh's house, and I will be with you.
Do not wait for me to come to you, for I am weak;
come to me yourself and capture me back from the unclean one who took me captive."
O soul, whom the world has corrupted with desires,
return and say boldly to the Lord your Lord:
"The Egyptian took me captive, corrupted my freedom and destroyed my purity,
and in the defilement of iniquity I dwelt with the wicked.
Mine is the blame, for had I not willed it, I would not have been led away;
his is the furnace, for he dared to carry off my freedom from you.
As for me, overlook it, and bring me out of the defilement in which I dwell;
as for him, hand him over and bind him, because he dared to lay hands on your possession.
As for me, heal me [?], for I have seen the house of the wicked;
and put him to shame, as the one he [?] is led away from him.
As for me, unite me with you, that I may be sanctified in you and be yours;
and him, cast him to destruction, to be food for the flame.
Pleasures sank me into his bed in the sleep of iniquity;
you, awaken me and lead me to you, and I will be with you."

Sin has held me back, and I cannot come to you;
come, take what is yours, so that I am not lost after you have found me.
Be pleased with me, I beg you, and mingle my will with your will,
and scorn the will of the tyrant, so that he may not rejoice over me.
I have become guilty because I sinned, ashamed because I strayed, and I have died because I chose it;
in you let me grow wise, in you let the shame of my face be forgotten.
The scent of life flows out from you, O King, my Savior;
come, bring me out of the stench in which I dwell.
The smell of death from the Egyptian has sickened me to the nostrils;
let your name be oil of myrrh to me, and let me be made fragrant by you.
The night of the world has darkened my paths and multiplied my stumblings;
come, O Day, Christ who created me, and let me be with you.
A snare met me and I fell into it, I who chose to fall;
come, break the snare and set free the soul that is strangled in it.
The Evil One with his snares has tripped up my good paths,
and see, I am walking on the road of iniquity: bring me to you.
Turn me back to yourself, Lord, as Sarah was returned to Abram,
and let me be sanctified by you and be yours here and hereafter.
Glory to you, Lord, for how greatly your mercy has loved me,
that you created me in mercy, discipline me in mercy, and make me your own in mercy.
